Z||000000||Cooter||02-09-2001||09:53 PM||||While scanning the Ebay adds, I saw this supercharger for a 2.5 liter Jeep. Did the Jeeps use the GM Duke? This would be one heck of a bolt on- if you could keep the crank in it!! <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=557662548" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=557662548</A> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||MrPBody||02-09-2001||09:58 PM||||I think some years of Cherokee (through '86?) came with a Duke or GM V-6.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hank is Here||02-09-2001||10:30 PM||||yep AMC did use the Duke 151.  It was in the CJ7 in the early 80's.  Different mounting points but basically the same motor.  AMC started using a 150, I forget who made this motor, in the mid 80's then.  Yep also the early Cherokees did use a carb'd 2.8 but never a fuel injected one, I think that they just started using the 258 carb'd in it's place.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||02-10-2001||10:22 PM||||My wife's mother used to have a 95 Wrangler with a four banger. It's been a while since I saw it, but if memory serves, it sure looked like a Duke.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||02-10-2001||11:17 PM||||Most of the AMC/Jeep product I've seen is heavily modified GM engines with assorted transmissions and New Process Gear transfer cases. NPG is a plant in Syracuse NY that is/was owned by Chrysler. For the longest time NPG made nearly all the transfer cases used in the U.S.<P>I would not expect anything that was used in an AMC/Jeep to fit a GM 2.5 w/o a bunch of issues.||||reg||1
